<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="rosmarinic acid biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5048" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; |FRAME: CPD-25183 &quot;Rosmarinic acid&quot;| (RA) is an ester of |FRAME: CAFFEATE| and |FRAME: CPD-6982|. It is widely spread in species of the |FRAME: TAX-4136| and |FRAME: TAX-21571| |CITS: [12482446]| and also occurs in some lower plants such as ferns and hornworts |CITS: [TAKEDA90]| |CITS: [12721853]|. It was first isolated in 1958 from rosemary (|FRAME: TAX-39367|), which is the source of its name. The 3&apos;-O-&amp;beta;-glucoside of RA (|FRAME:CPD-7018|) has been identified in the hornwort |FRAME: TAX-41834| |CITS: [16133208]|, where it accumulates up to 1% of the cell dry weight. Other rosmarinic acid derivatives of biological importance are |FRAME: CPD-26619 &quot;lithospermic acid&quot;|, a conjugate of rosmarinic acid and caffeic acid, and |FRAME: CPD-26620 &quot;lithospermic acid B&quot;, which is a dimer of rosmarinic acid |CITS: [12482446]|. Rosmarinic acid is used in foods or beverages as a flavoring, in cosmetics, and as a dietary supplement. Its biosynthesis has been studied extensively |CITS: [11943155]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The first report about the biosynthetic route of rosmarinic acid was published in 1970 |CITS: [5484678]| demonstrating the involvement of two aromatic amino acids, L-tyrosine and L-phenylalanine in the biogenesis of rosmarinate. Since then the entire pathway has been elucidated, mostly in |FRAME: TAX-4142| (|FRAME: TAX-4136|) including most of the involved enzymatic steps |CITS: [PETERSEN93]| |CITS: [PETERSEN93]|. Coumaroyl-CoA is one of the essential components being used for the assembly of rosmarinic acid. It is derived from L-phenylalanine in three well characterized enzymatic steps in plants catalyzed by L-phenylalanine-ammonia-lyase (PAL), cinnamic acid 4-hydroxylase (C4H) and 4-coumaric acid-CoA ligase (4CL) (compare |FRAME:PWY1F-467|). Some of those enzymes have been purified from lower plants like hornworts as well |CITS: 12721853[]| indicating that the general biosynthetic route of rosmarinic acid seems to be identical in higher and lower plants. The other metabolic branch contributing to the biosynthesis of RA originates from L-tyrosine resulting in the formation of 4-hydroxyphenyllactate (4HPL). The two moieties of the parallel pathways are connected by rosmarinic acid synthase (RAS) catalyzing a transesterification reaction |CITS: [PETERSEN88]| |CITS: [PETERSEN91]|. The RAS accepted caffeoyl-CoA and 3,4-dihydroxyphenyllactic acid (DHPL) resulting in the formation of rosmarinic acid. It should be noted that only the R(+)-stereoisomer of 3,4-dihydroxyphenyllactic acid is accepted by the RAS. On the other side 4-coumaroyl-CoA and either DHPL or its monohydroxylated isomer, 4-hydroxyphenyllactic acid (PHPL) was converted to their corresponding coumaroyl-hydroxyphenyllactates. The enzymatic capability of RAS and the precedent enzyme hydroxypuruvate reductase |CITS: [HAUSLER91]| |CITS: [15284489]| to accept different substrates (mono- or dihydroxylated forms of phenyllactates/phenylacetates) prompted the proposition for a potential second biosynthetic route for RA (|FRAME:PWY-5049|). The last steps in this pathway are catalyzed by two cytochrome P450 dependent monooxygenases introducing hydroxyl groups to the 3- and 3&apos;-position of 4-coumaroyl-4&apos;-hydroxyphenyllactate |CITS: [PETERSEN97]| |CITS: [11943155]|. Whereas the 3-hydroxylation of this hydroxycinnamic acid ester has been established the 3&apos;-hydroxylation has yet to be fully characterized.</Description>
